network, proxy_service: a name lookup is given three seconds, on the probe and on every upstream attempt

The reachability probe's HEAD and every upstream request carry a timeout,
and neither timeout covers the resolver. With a route on the interface and
no working DNS -- a handheld still associated to a hotspot whose uplink has
gone, or one whose Wi-Fi switch leaves the interface up -- glibc's resolver
gives up after about twenty seconds, and this process paid them on every
request while the tracker said unreachable: the request handler probed to
write online= in its log line before doing anything else, so a store-only
dorequest.php from the interface was answered twenty seconds after it
arrived, five after the interface had given up and gone to the web, which
timed out again ("An error occurred. Timeout was reached." on the RG35XX
SP, 2026-09-22, ROCKNIX fork #242). A game's launch paid twice: the log
line's probe, then login2's upstream attempt, before the store answered
the sign-in; the player saw no achievements for as long as that took.

Three changes. resolve_host_bounded looks a host up on a thread of its own
and gives it LOOKUP_SECONDS (3.0); the probe's HEAD and the upstream
forwarder call it first and report "unreachable" at once when it fails or
runs over, leaving a hung lookup to end on its daemon thread. And the
request log line reads the reachability tracker for every request rather
than probing -- the request's own path asks is_online() where the answer
decides something. Three tests: a lookup that hangs returns within the
bound, a failed lookup names its error, and the HEAD is never attempted
after a failed lookup.

Written for ROCKNIX (fork issue maxengel/rocknix#242). Offered upstream
as-is (ROCKNIX D-RA-002, D-RA-016).

